@short_title Distillery

{0 Eliom's Distillery}

The easiest way to initialize, compile, and run an Ocsigen/Eliom
project is to use [eliom-distillery].

[eliom-distillery] is self-documenting (refer to
[eliom-distillery -help] and the generated [README]), but here
is a bit of information on how to work with it.





{2 Project Initialization}

[eliom-distillery] creates projects from templates.
To see all available templates, you can use
{@shell[
$ eliom-distillery -list-templates
]}

Template "app" creates a simple client-server application project
(Dune-based build system and main service), with build rules generated
by {{:https://github.com/ocsigen/ocsigen-dune-rules}ocsigen-dune-rules}.
The project runs either as a static executable
{{!//workflow/page-configuration.no_config_file}without a configuration file},
whose paths (log directory, command pipe, static files and Ocsipersist
database file) are set in [app/main/main.ml], or as a library
dynamically loaded by ocsigenserver with the configuration file
[<name>.conf].

Alternatively, you might want to use the legacy "basic.ppx" template,
with a Makefile-based build system.

To create your project, do:

{@shell[
$ eliom-distillery -name <name> -template app [-target-directory <dir>]
]}

This creates a project named [<name>] from the ["app"]
template in the directory [<dir>] or [<name>] by default.
The project name should be a valid name for an OCaml compilation unit.

{2:compilation Compilation & Running}

Read the README file provided with the template.

{3 WebAssembly compilation}

The template [app] builds both a JavaScript and a WebAssembly version of
your client program. When WebAssembly is enabled (option [<wasm
enabled="true"/>] of the configuration file, or [~enable_wasm:true] for
{!Eliom.App.run}), the server detects the capabilities of the browser and
sends the appropriate version (see
{{!//workflow/page-configuration.wasm}WebAssembly}).

{2 Using other templates}

If you want to start from a more complete or more specific template,
you can get other templates from the internet, such as
Ocsigen Start. You can either install these templates into Eliom's own directory
([lib/eliom/templates/<name>], this is what their installer generally
does), or, if doing so is not practical, you can use the
[ELIOM_DISTILLERY_PATH] environment variable to point to your templates.
This environment variable is a colon-separated list of directories
containing templates (for example,
[export ELIOM_DISTILLERY_PATH=/usr/local/lib/mytemplates]).

A template's name is the name of its directory: with
[/usr/local/lib/mytemplates/template1], if you want to create a new
project based on [template1], you can use

{@shell[
$ eliom-distillery -name example -template template1
]}

{2 Write other templates}

You can also write your own templates and install them by using the
previously described method.

By default, [eliom-distillery] copies all files in the template
directory. If you want to ignore some files, you can list them in the
special file [.eliomignore].

For some templates, e.g., the one of Ocsigen Start, some project
names are reserved to avoid conflicts. For a new template, you need to
list all reserved project names in the special file
[.eliomreserve].

[eliom-distillery] also replaces [%%%PROJECT_NAME%%%] (resp.
[%%%MODULE_NAME%%%]) by the project name (resp. by the project
name with the first letter capitalized).

For examples, see pre-defined templates like [app], [basic.ppx] and [os] (from
Ocsigen Start).
